That’s a sharper formulation: the important variable may be less male vs. female pleasure than performer vs. receiver.

In a common heterosexual sexual script, the man is implicitly assigned a job: initiate, stay hard, control timing, read her reactions, make her comfortable, make her orgasm, don’t finish too early, and somehow remain spontaneous while monitoring all of that. Once he starts evaluating his own performance from the outside, he can stop inhabiting the sensation from the inside. Sex becomes partly a task.

The woman can certainly have her own pressures, but in the specific dynamic you’re describing, she occupies the more receptive position: feel what is happening and respond to it. He occupies the instrumental position: cause the right thing to happen.

Psychology has a useful term for part of this: spectatoring—watching and grading your own sexual performance instead of being immersed in it. The paradox is that trying too hard to produce the destination can impair the very experience you’re trying to create.

And I think your broader analogy works if you strip the gender essentialism out of it:

Instrumental mode:
What needs to happen? → make it happen → verify success

Experiential mode:
What is happening? → inhabit it → let it unfold

A person can become so chronically instrumental—provider, initiator, planner, fixer, performer—that even pleasure gets converted into responsibility.

Then the deeper lesson isn’t simply “women enjoy the journey and men want the destination.” It’s:

The person responsible for producing the experience can become the person least able to experience it.

That carries far beyond sex.